%I A400012 #19 Nov 27 2026 16:55:13
%S A400012 3911962129,3911962169,3911962237,3911962247,3911962309,3911962319,
%T A400012 3911962393,3911962409,3911962493,3911962519,3911962547,3911962631,
%U A400012 3911962661,3911962669,3911962699,3911962849,3911962853,3911962861,3911962903,3911962927,3911962961,3911962979
%N A400012 Primes p for which pi_{120;73,97}(p) - pi_{120;1,49}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A400012 Let H be a proper subgroup of (Z/NZ)* containing all the squares, and S != H be a coset of H. Assuming the generalized Riemann hypothesis (GRH) and the Grand Simplicity Hypothesis (GSH; called LI in page 29 of Greg Martin's 2010 link), then pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x) occurs more often than not: the set {pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x)} has logarithmic density greater than 1/2. This phenomenon is called "Chebyshev's bias".
%C A400012 Chebyshev's bias can also be seen from the perspective of Dirichlet characters. Let T be a nontrivial coset of a nontrivial subgroup of real Dirichlet characters modulo N. Let H be the subgroup of (Z/NZ)* of elements r such that chi(r) = 1 for all chi in T, and S be the coset of H consisting of elements r such that chi(r) = -1 for all chi in T, then pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x) occurs more often than not.
%C A400012 Here we have N = 120, H = {1,49} and S = {73,97}, corresponding to T = {chi_5, chi_{-15}, chi_{-20}, chi_40, chi_60, chi_120, chi_{-40}, chi_{-120}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 120.
%C A400012 Assuming GRH and LI, the set of x with pi_{120;73,97}(x) > pi_{120;1,49}(x) has logarithmic density 0.99830, making the race between {1,49} and {73,97} the least biased among the 15 races between cosets of index 16 in (Z/120Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of subgroups containing the squares modulo 120" below. Along with the second least biased index-16 race between {1,49} and {61,109} (A400013), these are the only two races with known sign-changing zones below x0 = 7*10^12.
%C A400012 Let D(x) = pi_{120;73,97}(x) - pi_{120;1,49}(x). (We have D(x0) = 88031; for the concrete numbers of primes below x0 in each congruence class modulo 120, see the file "pi_mod120_at_7e12.csv" in the Zenodo preprint). There are two sign-changing zones giving a total of 32312 terms below x0:
%C A400012   (1) a(1..8977) = 3911962129-3966978187 (there are 1826536 primes a(1) <= p <= a(8977) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -211 at p = 3915938449);
%C A400012   (2) a(8978..32312) = 1703677539649-1704350185283 (there are 9088841 primes a(8978) <= p <= a(32312) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -880 at p = 1703870700289).
%C A400012 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 10915377.
%H A400012 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A400012/b400012.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..32312</a> (all terms below 7*10^12)
%H A400012 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24516487">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of subgroups containing the squares modulo 120</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A400012 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A400012 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A400012 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A400012 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A400012 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%120==1||p%120==49, k--); if(p%120==73||p%120==97, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A400012 Cf. A400013, A402745, A402746.
%Y A400012 See A401958 for races between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*.
